1. ArrayList Vs LinkedList

Feature	                            ArrayList	                            LinkedList
📦 Internal Data Structure	        Dynamic array (resizable array)	        Doubly linked list
⚡ Access (get/set)	                ✅ Fast (O(1))	                        ❌ Slow (O(n)) — must traverse nodes
➕ Insert at end	                ✅ Fast (amortized O(1))	            ✅ Fast (O(1))
➕ Insert at middle/start	        ❌ Slower (O(n)) — shift elements	    ✅ Faster (O(1) if pointer known)
❌ Remove from middle/start	        ❌ Slower (O(n)) — shift elements	    ✅ Fast (O(1) if pointer known)
📊 Memory use	                    Less (only data)	                    More (stores data + 2 pointers)
🔄 Iteration	                    ✅ Fast — array indexed	                ⚠️ Slightly slower
🔍 Search by index	                ✅ O(1)	                                ❌ O(n)

ArrayList
    a. Uses a dynamic array
    b. When full, it creates a new array with 50% more size, and copies elements over

LinkedList
    a. Since LinkedList.size() isn’t cached, it gets recalculated in every iteration

🚫 Common Misunderstanding
“LinkedList is O(1) for insert at any position”

❌ Only true if you already have the node reference
✅ Not true for index-based operations (which require traversal)

2. Symmetric Encryption vs Asymmetric Encryption

Feature	                            Symmetric Encryption	                            Asymmetric Encryption
🔑 Keys	                    Same key for encryption and decryption	                Key pair: Public key + Private key
📦 Speed	                Faster, lightweight	                                    Slower, computationally heavy
📬 Key Sharing	            Requires secure key exchange	                        Public key can be shared openly
🔒 Security Risk	        Key compromise affects both encryption/decryption	    Private key must be protected
💡 Typical Usage	        Encrypting large data files or secure channels	        Initial handshakes, certificates, email encryption

