4. Spring @Transaction Internal Working

       When you annotate a method with @Transactional, Spring doesn’t change your method itself. Instead, it creates a proxy object that wraps your original bean.
       When you call transactional method from outside the class, Spring routes the call through a proxy, which:
           Starts a transaction
           Invokes the original method
           Commits or rolls back
       Key Components Involved
           @Transactional	Declarative marker
           TransactionInterceptor	Core AOP logic
           PlatformTransactionManager	Starts, commits, rolls back transactions
           DataSourceTransactionManager	JDBC transactions (for RDBMS)
           JpaTransactionManager	JPA/Hibernate transactions

       [Client Code]
            ↓
        [Spring Proxy] ← @Transactional logic (starts tx)
            ↓
       [Target Method] ← Your actual logic
            ↓
        [Spring Proxy] ← commits or rolls back

5. IPV4 vs IPV6

Feature	                            IPv4	                    IPv6
Address Size	                    32-bit	                    128-bit
Address Format	            Decimal (e.g., 192.168.0.1)	        Hexadecimal (e.g., 2001:0db8:85a3::8a2e:0370:7334)
Address Space	            ~4.3 billion addresses	            ~340 undecillion (3.4×10³⁸) addresses
Header Size	                20 bytes	                        40 bytes (more efficient design)
Security	                Optional (IPSec optional)	        Built-in (IPSec is mandatory)
Checksum	                Yes	                                No (removed to improve speed)
Adoption	                Widely used	                        Growing, still not universally adopted

6. Database sharding - Splitting one big database into smaller, faster, more manageable pieces (shards), each handling part of the data.
   Each shard contains a subset of the data, and all shards together make up the complete dataset.

   Types of Sharding
   Horizontal Sharding	=> Split rows by a key (user ID, region, etc.) ->	Users with ID 1–1000 → Shard 1
   Vertical Sharding	=> Split tables or columns ->	User data in DB1, Orders in DB2
   Directory-Based	=> Uses a lookup table to find the right shard	-> Central metadata maps keys to shards

