8. @Component & @Bean
    Both are used to register beans in the Spring container, but they are used in different ways and contexts.

    @Component
        - Used for class-level annotations.
        - Automatically detects and registers classes annotated with @Component during component scanning.
        - Ideal for classes that are part of your application logic (e.g., services, repositories).

    @Bean
        - Used for method-level annotations.
        - Explicitly defines a bean in a configuration class.
        - Ideal for third-party libraries or when you need to customize bean creation.

9. Spring Boot vs Micronaut
   Feature	                                    Spring Boot	                                                Micronaut
   🏁 Startup Time	                Slower (due to runtime reflection)	                Very fast (no reflection, compile-time DI)
   🔧 Dependency Injection	        Runtime via reflection (@Autowired)	                Compile-time using annotation processors
   🪞 Reflection Usage	            Heavy (Spring uses reflection a lot)	            Avoids reflection entirely
   🧠 Memory Usage	                Higher (due to reflection, proxies)	                Lower footprint
   ☁️ Cloud & Microservices	    Mature, wide support (Spring Cloud, Netflix OSS)	    Built for microservices from scratch
   🔌 Plugins & Extensions	    Huge ecosystem (Spring Security, Data, etc.)	        Growing ecosystem
   🧰 Tooling & Community	        Very large community, excellent tooling	            Smaller but active community
   🧪 Learning Curve	            Easier if you know Spring ecosystem	                Simpler for new projects, less boilerplate

   🔥 Performance Comparison
   Startup Time	                                    1–3 seconds	                                        ~100–300 ms
   Memory Usage	                                    Higher (100MB+)	                                    Lower (30–50MB)

   ☁️ Ideal Use Cases
   Large enterprise app (banking, healthcare)   =>	Spring Boot
   Serverless, microservices, low-latency apps	=>  Micronaut
   Rapid development with tons of integrations	=>  Spring Boot
   Lightweight APIs or native images	        =>  Micronaut
